\[\left\{\begin{aligned} a_{r}(\mathbf{u}_{r}(q),\mathbf{v})& =\langle\mathbf{f},\mathbf{v}\rangle\,+\,(\operatorname{div} \mathbf{v},q)&\quad\forall\mathbf{v}\in H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{N},\\ \mathbf{u}_{r}(q)&=\mathbf{g}&\quad \text{on}\,\,\Gamma,\end{aligned}\right.\]

we find in view of (5.23):

\[a_{r}(D\mathbf{u}_{r}\cdot\mu,\mathbf{v}) =(\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v},\mu)\quad\quad\forall\mathbf{v}\in H ^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{N},\] \[D\mathbf{u}_{r}\cdot\mu =\mathbf{0}\quad\text{on}\,\,\Gamma,\] \[DK_{r}(q) =\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}_{r}(q),\] \[D^{2}K_{r}\cdot\mu =\operatorname{div}(D\mathbf{u}_{r}\cdot\mu).\]

Hence the _simple gradient algorithm with optimal parameter_ reads as follows:

\(1^{\circ})\) Given \(p^{0}\in L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\), solve the non-homogeneous elliptic boundary value problem:

\[\left\{\begin{aligned} -(\nu\mathcal{A}\,+\,r\,\mathbf{grad} \operatorname{div})\mathbf{u}^{0}&=\mathbf{f}-\,\mathbf{grad} \,p^{0}&\quad\text{in}\,\,\Omega,\\ \mathbf{u}^{0}&=\mathbf{g}&\quad\text{on} \,\,\Gamma.\end{aligned}\right.\]

\(2^{\circ})\) For \(m\geqslant 0\), knowing \((\mathbf{u}^{m},p^{m})\) in \(H^{1}(\Omega)^{N}\times L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) with \(\mathbf{u}^{m}|_{\Gamma}=\mathbf{g}\), solve the homogeneous problem:

\[\left\{\begin{aligned} -(\nu\mathcal{A}\,+\,r\,\mathbf{grad} \operatorname{div})\mathbf{z}^{m}&=\mathbf{grad}\operatorname{ div}\mathbf{u}^{m}&\quad\text{in}\,\,\Omega,\\ \mathbf{z}^{m}&=\mathbf{0}&\quad\text{on} \,\,\Gamma;\end{aligned}\right.\]

then compute \(\rho^{m}\in\mathbb{R}\) and the next pair \((\mathbf{u}^{m+1},p^{m+1})\in H^{1}(\Omega)^{N}\times L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) by:

\[\rho^{m} =-\|\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m}\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}/( \operatorname{div}\mathbf{z}^{m},\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m}),\] \[p^{m+1} =p^{m}-\rho^{m}\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m},\] \[\mathbf{u}^{m+1} =\mathbf{u}^{m}+\rho^{m}\mathbf{z}^{m}.\]

It can be readily checked that the statement of Theorem 4.6 holds, namely the above scheme is always convergent. This is still true when \(\rho^{m}\) is not the optimal parameter, provided that

\[0<\inf_{m}\rho^{m}\leqslant\sup_{m}\rho^{m}<2\tilde{\alpha}_{r}.\]

A simple calculation shows that

\[\tilde{\alpha}_{r}\leqslant r+v/N.\]

The _conjugate-gradient algorithm_ has the same starting procedure \(n^{\circ}1\) while step \(n^{\circ}2\) is replaced by:

\(2^{\circ})\) For \(m\geqslant 0\), knowing \((\mathbf{u}^{m},p^{m})\) in \(H^{1}(\Omega)^{N}\times L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) with \(\mathbf{u}^{m}|_{\Gamma}=\mathbf{g}\), compute \(\sigma^{m}\in\mathbb{R}\) and \(\omega^{m}\in L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) by:

\[\sigma^{m} =\|\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m}\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2}/\| \operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m-1}\|_{0,\,\Omega}^{2},\] \[\omega^{m} =\operatorname{div}\mathbf{u}^{m}+\sigma^{m}\omega^{m-1}, \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\